## How it works

The assignment roster defines the experiment population. A unit with no events
in the outcome window remains in the denominator of supported mean outcomes.
The same calculation supplies the headless result and this fixed result view;
changing a display tab or height never draws another bootstrap sample.

The effect direction is treatment minus control. Cumulative ordered reach is a
binary outcome over all assignments. Conditional transitions use the previous
reached-step population, which can itself be changed by treatment.

Intervals carry their method and availability status. Percentile bootstrap draws
whole independent units inside their assigned arm. Explicit Newcombe binary
intervals use Wilson arm intervals; there is no hidden method substitution.
All intervals in this version are pointwise.

See [Experiments](/docs/experiments) for population, observation, metric and
inference contracts, and the end-to-end tutorial. This initial widget displays a
calculated snapshot; assignment-table state restoration is not implemented.
